    "narrative": "For July 31, our primary objective is capitalizing on high-ceiling matchups to close the gap in stolen bases and counting stats. **Corbin Carroll** leads the charge with an elite 84.02 score, buoyed by a favorable matchup against Bibee. His ability to anchor the top of the lineup remains our most reliable source of production. Joining him in the \"Strong\" tier is **Mickey Moniak**, who sits at a 75.74 thanks to a 14% boost from a favorable platoon advantage against Wacha and a significant park factor, while **Brice Turang** earns a 73.39 score, leveraging his leadoff spot and a 19% boost from opposing pitcher skill to remain a vital cog for our SB-starved roster.\n\nThe most challenging decision today was leaving **Austin Riley** on the bench. His 50.74 score made him the closest candidate for a starting slot, but he ultimately lost out to **Isaac Paredes** (51.07) due to the latter's slightly superior platoon profile against Eovaldi and the need to preserve our daily roster efficiency. Meanwhile, we must remain vigilant regarding our \"Lineup Pending\" status for **Josh Naylor**, **Brice Turang**, **Cole Young**, **Zach McKinstry**, **Tyler Soderstrom**, and **Henry Bolte**. Specifically, while **Cole Young** benefits from a slow opposing pitcher delivery that boosts his SB upside, he remains a marginal play at 54.86. We are also monitoring **Jose Caballero**; despite a sub-60 score, his 13% platoon edge against Imanaga keeps him relevant, though the 8% penalty for batting ninth is a drag on his overall output. With **Vinnie Pasquantino** officially on the IL, our depth is being tested, making every start count as we look to maximize our total category contributions.",
